      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Supporters argue that substantive due process is vital because it allows the Constitution to evolve and protect rights that the Founders could not have foreseen, like modern privacy. However, critics argue that it is dangerous because it allows judges to impose their own political views onto the law, rather than strictly interpreting the text. This debate highlights the tension between judicial activism in protecting liberties and the need for predictable, text-based constitutional adherence.",
      "source": "target",
